{{smaller block|In studying the minor poets, we see with especial clearness the ''adverse'' influences of a transition era, composite though it be.{{right|''Stedman,'' Vict. Poets, p. 28.}}}}
'''4.''' In ''bot.'', turned toward the axis: the opposite of ''averse'', but rarely used. See ''anatropous''. [The early botanists used the term in the sense of ''opposite''.]—'''Adverse leaf''', {{smaller|a leaf which has its upper surface turned toward the stem.—}}'''Adverse possession''', {{smaller|in ''law'', occupancy of realty as if by right without molestation, which may at length ripen into a sufficient title.—}}'''Adverse radicle''', {{smaller|in ''bot.'', a radicle turned toward the hilum, as in anatropous seeds. See ''anatropous''.}} = '''Syn.''' {{smaller|'''1.''' Opposite, contrary, unfavorable.—'''2.''' ''Averse, Inimical,'' etc. See ''hostile''.—'''3.''' Unfortunate, unlucky, calamitous, untoward, disastrous.}}

'''adverse†''' (ad-vėrs′), ''v. t.'' [< L. ''adversari'', oppose, < ''adversus'', opposite: see ''adverse'', ''a.''] To oppose.
{{smaller block|Fortune should him ''adverse''.{{right|''Gower,'' Conf. Amant., ii.}}}}

'''adversely''' (ad′vėrs-li), ''adv.'' In an adverse manner; oppositely; inimically; offensively; unfortunately; unprosperously; in a manner contrary to desire or success.
{{smaller block|If the drink you give me touch my palate ''adversely'', I make a crooked face at it.{{right|''Shak.,'' Cor., ii. 1.}}}}

'''adverseness''' (ad′vėrs-nes), ''n.'' '''1.''' Opposition; repugnance.
{{smaller block|This would account for an ''adverseness'' to all our overtures for peace.{{right|''Hallam.''}}}}
'''2.''' Adversity; unprosperousness: as, ''adverseness'' of circumstances.

'''adversifoliate''' (ad-vėr-si-fō′li-ạ̄t), ''a.'' [< L. ''adversus'', opposite, + ''folium'', leaf, + ''-ate''<sup>1</sup>.] In ''bot.'', having opposite leaves: applied to plants where the leaves are arranged opposite to each
